Rezona Platform Assessment: Technical Specification Methodology

Performance Report – Versions V1 to V4.2

• 1. Executive Summary

Rezona is an emerging AI-powered game generation platform with significant potential. Its current capabilities support rapid prototyping of casual games and memeplays. However, there is a measurable ceiling beyond which prompt detail does not translate into functional complexity. This report documents the performance of technical specifications across four versions, identifies the current complexity threshold, and provides recommendations for platform evolution.

• 2. Methodology

Games were developed using a structured specification format inspired by industry documentation standards (Microsoft, NVIDIA). Each version increased functional complexity:

Version Core Features Result
V1 Descent from 40,000 altitude, asteroid avoidance Fully functional, single iteration
V2 Landing cutscene (SVG animation) Fully functional, single iteration
V3 Surface exploration, passive resource spawn Fully functional, single iteration
V4 Active controls (S/J/A/I), inventory, crafting Partial functionality, multiple iterations
V4.1 Joystick visibility, mode switching Partial functionality, in progress
V4.2 Full crafting system with permanent upgrades Not yet functional

• 3. Key Findings

• 3.1 Detailed Specifications Work (Up to a Point)

V1, V2, and V3 were generated successfully on the first attempt using structured specifications with clear rules, cooldowns, and visual descriptions. The AI understood and implemented:

· Altitude counters and integrity bars
· Asteroid spawning with movement patterns
· Cinematic transitions with SVG animations
· Resource spawning and passive collection

• 3.2 Complexity Ceiling Identified

V4 introduced:

· Four active buttons with individual cooldowns (S/J/A/I)
· Inventory modal with real-time material tracking
· Crafting system with permanent upgrade effects
· Joystick and button mode switching (DESCENT vs. SURFACE_EXPLORATION)

The AI generated code with correct structure but failed on:

· Persistent cooldown timing
· Inventory state management
· Button response consistency
· Joystick visibility across mode transitions

• 3.3 Platform Limitations vs. Spec Quality

The specifications are not the bottleneck. V4 documents a complete, logical system. The AI understands the intent but cannot execute it reliably. This indicates a platform limitation, not a prompt engineering failure.

• 4. Platform Strengths (Acknowledged)

· Zero-code generation from natural language
· Fast iteration (seconds per generation)
· SVG asset generation from text descriptions
· Built-in multiplayer support (recent addition)
· Strong social features (remixing, sharing, comments)

Rezona is excellent for:

· Memeplays (15–90 second experiences)
· Casual games with simple mechanics
· Rapid prototyping and community testing
· Onboarding non-technical creators

• 5. Recommended Platform Evolution

To support games with V4 complexity, Rezona would need:

Feature Priority
Persistent state management (variables that survive scene changes) High
Cooldown system with visual indicators High
Modal UI support (inventory, crafting menus) Medium
Joystick visibility forced above cinematic layers Low
Memory between prompt iterations (session context) Medium

These features would enable:

· Survival mechanics with resource management
· Crafting and upgrade systems
· Persistent player progression
· Complex control schemes

• 6. Conclusion

Rezona has real potential. The platform works well for casual and meme-driven content. With targeted improvements in state management and UI layering, it could support medium-complexity games currently possible only in traditional engines or more flexible AI environments.

The specification methodology documented in this report is available under MIT license at the official repository. It demonstrates what Rezona can do today and what it could do tomorrow with continued development.

